WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:06.220
Making big-budget blockbuster movies is a
complicated and insanely expensive endeavor.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.530
And keeping the Hollywood machine running
sometimes requires lengthy contracts to ensure

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:15.329
that the famous faces that attract theater-goers
keep making their magic on the big screen.

00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:19.780
But this means sometimes the high-profile
actors attached to these projects are actually

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.790
working against their will, contractually
obligated to take on a role they'd rather

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:25.790
not.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:26.790
Actors such as ...

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.640
Jennifer Garner in Elektra

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:34.239
After the financial success of 2003's Daredevil,
attention turned to a spin-off focusing on

00:00:34.239 --> 00:00:36.579
Jennifer Garner's character, Elektra.

00:00:36.579 --> 00:00:41.149
Daredevil writer and director Mark Steven
Johnson discussed bringing Kevin Smith on

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:45.890
board to help with the screenplay, and Garner
gushed in interviews about possible paths

00:00:45.890 --> 00:00:49.999
the storyline could take in the sequel she
was already under contract to complete.

00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:55.710
Unfortunately, sometimes even the best-laid
plans go south, and 2005's Elektra didn't

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:57.590
live up to anyone's expectations.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:02.049
Johnson was replaced as the writer and director,
the film bombed at the box office and was

00:01:02.049 --> 00:01:06.980
panned by audiences and critics, and Garner
herself even told her ex-boyfriend Michael

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.330
Vartan that the movie was "awful."

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:14.280
In an interview with US Weekly, Vartan explained,
"She had to do it because of Daredevil.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:16.440
It was in her contract."

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.290
Mike Myers in The Cat in the Hat

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:23.180
Following his first Austin Powers movie, Universal
signed Mike Myers to appear in what was to

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.050
be a comedy based on his "Dieter" character
from Saturday Night Live.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:32.040
"I'm so full of anticipation that my genitals
have sucked up into my body cavity."

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:36.280
He backed out of the part due to issues with
the screenplay, which launched a series of

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.100
legal battles between the studio and the star.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.500
Eventually, the sides reached an agreement—one
that obligated Myers to play the title character

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.980
in a live-action adaptation of Dr. Seuss'
The Cat in the Hat.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:53.310
Myers reportedly made no effort to play nice
while on the set; his co-star Amy Hill revealed

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.000
that he acted like a "hermit" and a "diva"
throughout the whole production.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.730
The end result was so underwhelming that Dr.
Seuss' widow, Audrey Geisel, stated in 2004

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:07.150
that she'd never allow Hollywood to make another
live-action adaptation of one of his books

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:08.150
again.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:11.370
Jessica Alba in Fantastic Four: Rise of the
Silver Surfer

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:15.390
When you agree to play a major character in
a superhero movie, you'd better be prepared

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:19.160
to play that same role again in the future—even
if the movie is a flop.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.970
This was the case for Jessica Alba, who played
Fantastic Four team member Sue Storm in the

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.400
2005 Fantastic Four movie.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.610
Because of her three-movie contract, Alba
was obligated to return for the 2007 sequel,

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.090
Rise of the Silver Surfer—an experience
she hated so much that she actually considered

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:38.170
quitting acting for good.

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:40.810
Daniel Craig in Skyfall and Spectre

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:46.180
He's been enormously successful in his role
as British spy James Bond, but Daniel Craig

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:50.500
has always been brutally honest about why
he hates playing the character—and why he

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:51.530
keeps doing it.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.340
After the release of Skyfall in 2012, Craig
admitted that he was never keen on becoming

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.720
Bond in the first place, saying, "I've been
trying to get out of this from the very moment

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.870
I got into it."

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:07.380
During the 2015 promotional tour for Spectre,
Craig turned the Bond hatred up a notch, responding

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:12.040
to the prospect of more sequels by saying
he'd rather "slash [his] wrists" than appear

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.290
in another Bond film, and saying if he did,
it would just be "for the money."

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.720
In July 2017, the Mirror reported that Craig
will indeed cash in and stay on to play Bond

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:26.500
for the fifth time, despite his well-publicized
disdain for the part.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.780
Roy Scheider in Jaws 2

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:34.520
He rose to worldwide fame with his role as
Police Chief Martin Brody in 1975's Jaws,

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:39.850
but Roy Scheider wasn't particularly excited
to reprise his role in the 1978 sequel.

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:44.380
So why was he the only member of the original
cast to come back for the second Jaws film?

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:46.180
Simple: he was contractually obliged.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:49.810
"I'm telling you and I'm telling everybody
at this table that that's a shark.

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.129
I know what a shark looks like, because I've
seen one up close.

00:03:53.129 --> 00:03:56.300
And you better do something about this one,
because I don't intend to go through that

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:57.300
hell again!"

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:01.530
Scheider had signed a multiple-movie contract
with Universal Studios, and they'd originally

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:04.870
cast him as the lead in 1978's The Deer Hunter.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:09.760
Unhappy with the script, he dropped out of
the film, and was replaced by Robert De Niro—but

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:14.320
because of that contract, Universal was able
to force Scheider to reprise his role as Chief

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:16.209
Brody in Jaws 2.

00:04:16.209 --> 00:04:18.130
Marlon Brando in Désirée

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:22.730
You would think Hollywood legend Marlon Brando
always got a say in which movies he made.

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:27.460
But even Brando had to deal with contractual
obligations—perhaps most infamously when,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:33.520
as a result of a lawsuit filed by a producer,
he was forced to appear as Napoleon in 1954's

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:34.520
Désirée.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.390
But Brando didn't take it lying down.

00:04:36.390 --> 00:04:41.230
According to one biography, he made a point
of "forgetting his lines or reciting them

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:47.040
with a nasal pseudo-British intonation and
creating havoc between takes," including "squirting

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.680
extras with a fire hose."

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:53.250
According to Brando's costar, the late Jean
Simmons, upon the film's release, Brando was

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:58.780
amazed at the movie's success, which included
two Oscar nominations, one for art direction

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:00.550
and the other for design.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:04.600
Had Brando put some effort into his role,
he probably could have garnered a nomination

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:05.600
for himself ...

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:07.790
"I coulda been a contender!

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:10.560
I coulda been somebody."

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.560
Thanks for watching!

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.510
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:17.020
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

